{"id":"milan","slug":"milan","name":"MILAN","aliases":["MILAN 2","Milan ATGM","Milan missile","Milan Anti-Tank Weapon","Milan anti tank missile"],"kind":"missile","category":"atgms","subcategory":"Franco-German anti-tank missile","description":"MILAN is a Franco-West German anti-tank guided missile system. Design of the MILAN began in 1962; it was ready for trials in 1971, and accepted for service in 1972. It is a wire-guided semi-automatic command to line of sight (SACLOS) missile, which means the sight of the launch unit must be aimed at a target to guide the missile. The MILAN can be equipped with a MIRA or MILIS thermal sight to give it night-firing ability.","history":"MILAN is a product of Euromissile, a Franco-West German missile development program dating back to the 1960s. The system entered service in 1972 as a second generation anti-tank weapon and soon became a standard anti-tank weapon throughout NATO, in use by most of the alliance's individual armies.\nConsisting of two main components, the launcher and the missile, the MILAN system uses a semi-automatic command to line of sight (SACLOS) command guidance system. It tracks the missile either by a tail-mounted infrared lamp or an electronic-flash lamp, depending on the model. Because it is guided by wire by an operator, the missile cannot be affected by radio jamming or flares. However, drawbacks include short range, exposure of the operator, and problems with overland powerlines.\nThe MILAN 2 variant, which entered service with the French, German and British armies in 1984, uses an improved 115 mm high-explosive anti-tank (HEAT) shaped charge warhead. The MILAN 3 entered service with the French army in 1995 and features a new-generation localizer that makes the system more difficult to jam electronically.","facts":[],"specifications":[{"group":"Warhead","items":[{"label":"Detonation Mechanism","value":"contact"}]},{"group":"Propulsion & Guidance","items":[{"label":"Engine","value":"solid-fuel rocket"},{"label":"Guidance","value":"SACLOS wire"},{"label":"Steering","value":"Jet deflector"},{"label":"Launch Platform","value":"Individual, vehicle"},{"label":"Launch Platform","value":"<!--"}]},{"group":"Dimensions","items":[{"label":"Weight","value":"16.4 kg","numeric":16.4,"unit":"kg"},{"label":"Length","value":"1.2 m","numeric":1.2,"unit":"m"},{"label":"Diameter","value":"115 mm","numeric":115,"unit":"mm"},{"label":"Wingspan","value":"260 mm","numeric":260,"unit":"mm"}]},{"group":"Performance","items":[{"label":"Operational Range","value":"200–2,000 m;"},{"label":"Operational Range","value":"3,000 m (MILAN ER)","numeric":3000,"unit":"m"},{"label":"Speed","value":"200 m/s","numeric":200,"unit":"m/s"}]},{"group":"Production","items":[{"label":"Designed","value":"1970s"},{"label":"Manufacturer","value":"MBDA"},{"label":"Manufacturer (Also produced under license by)","value":"Bharat Dynamics (India)"},{"label":"Manufacturer (Also produced under license by)","value":"BAe Dynamics (United Kingdom)"},{"label":"Produced","value":"1971"},{"label":"Number Built","value":"350,000 missiles, 10,000 launchers"},{"label":"Unit Cost","value":"£7,500 (1984)"}]},{"group":"Service","items":[{"label":"Origin","value":"France, West Germany"},{"label":"In Service","value":"1972–present"}]}],"specIndex":{"Detonation Mechanism":"contact","Engine":"solid-fuel rocket","Guidance":"SACLOS wire","Steering":"Jet deflector","Launch Platform":"Individual, vehicle","Weight":"16.4 kg","Length":"1.2 m","Diameter":"115 mm","Wingspan":"260 mm","Operational Range":"200–2,000 m;","Speed":"200 m/s","Designed":"1970s","Manufacturer":"MBDA","Produced":"1971","Number Built":"350,000 missiles, 10,000 launchers","Unit Cost":"£7,500 (1984)","Origin":"France, West Germany","In Service":"1972–present"},"images":{"hero":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/65/Tag_der_Bundeswehr_Jagel_2019_HJL_13_noBG.png","width":3000,"height":2218,"license":"See Wikimedia Commons"},"thumb":"https://upload.wiki